    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Two jobs: camping vs storage</h2>
<p>An RV security camera has very different requirements depending on whether you are staying with the rig or leaving it. Most guides blur the two, which is how people end up with a Wi-Fi camera sitting uselessly in a storage lot with no network to join.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>While camping</th><th>In storage</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Main threats</td><td>Theft of bikes, generators, grills, chairs; campsite visitors; wildlife</td><td>Break-ins, catalytic converter and battery theft, vandalism, rodent and water damage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>RV Wi-Fi router, phone hotspot, campground Wi-Fi</td><td>4G SIM in the camera (no Wi-Fi available)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power</td><td>Built-in solar, battery, or 12 V house battery</td><td>Solar only (house battery will drain over months)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mounting</td><td>Temporary, removable every move</td><td>Semi-permanent, theft-resistant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recording</td><td>Motion clips while you sleep or are away for the day</td><td>Motion clips over weeks or months, with remote battery checks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Pick your main use first. If you need both, a 4G camera with Wi-Fi fallback covers storage and camping, but it costs more and carries a data plan.</p>

<h2>RV security camera solar setups while camping</h2>
<p>At a campground, you are usually within 10–20 m of the camera, and you probably already have connectivity for your phone and laptop. That makes the setup simple.</p>
<h3>Connect to your RV router or hotspot</h3>
<p>Most battery and solar cameras join a 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi network. If you run a mobile router in the RV, set the camera up on that network once and it will reconnect at every site, since the network name and password stay the same. If you use your phone as a hotspot, remember the camera only works when the phone is in range with hotspot on, which defeats the purpose when you drive into town.</p>
<p>Campground Wi-Fi is the weakest option. Many campgrounds use <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captive_portal" rel="noopener" target="_blank">captive portals</a> (a login page), which cameras cannot get past, and the network changes at every stop. Our guide to <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-wifi/">whether solar cameras need Wi-Fi</a> explains what happens when the connection drops.</p>
<h3>Local-only recording</h3>
<p>If you only want a record of what happened overnight, a camera with a microSD card records without any internet. You review clips in the morning by connecting your phone to the camera directly (where the model supports it) or once you are back on Wi-Fi. No alerts, but no data costs either.</p>
<h3>Power while camping</h3>
<p>A camera with a built-in panel recharges on its own if you park it in sun. At wooded sites, a separate panel on a longer cable can sit on the roof or a sunny spot while the camera stays in the shade. If you camp for a week or two at a time, a fully charged camera battery often lasts that long on light duty anyway. For longer stays, our <a href="/guides/portable-solar-camera-camping/">portable solar camera camping guide</a> covers power banks and portable power stations.</p>

<h2>Camper security camera solar setups in storage</h2>
<p>Storage is where solar earns its keep. An RV can sit for three to six months in a lot with no power hookups, and the house battery will be flat within weeks if you leave anything running on it. A <strong>camper security camera solar</strong> unit with its own panel and SIM is the reliable answer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choose 4G LTE.</strong> Storage lots rarely offer Wi-Fi. A 4G camera with a nano-SIM uploads clips and alerts from anywhere with signal. See our <a href="/reviews/solar-cctv-camera-with-sim-card/">solar CCTV camera with SIM card review</a> for what matters in a cellular model.</li>
<li><strong>Check signal in the lot.</strong> Metal-roofed covered storage and rows of tall rigs can weaken signal. Test with a phone on the same carrier at the mounting height.</li>
<li><strong>Size the data plan.</strong> A storage camera sees little activity, often just a handful of clips a week. A small plan of 1–3 GB a month is usually enough. Run your numbers through the <a href="/tools/4g-data-and-storage-calculator/">4G data and storage calculator</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the panel’s sun.</strong> Covered RV storage (carports and barns) can block the sky entirely. Put the panel on the roof of the RV, on the carport’s sunny edge, or on a fence if the lot allows.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Ask the storage facility first</strong><p>Many lots allow cameras on your own rig; some ban anything attached to fences or posts, and some prohibit cameras that record other customers’ vehicles. Aim the camera at your RV, not the whole row, and check the rules in your rental agreement.</p></div>
<p>An <strong>RV storage lot security camera</strong> should point at the entry door and the underside of the rig (where catalytic converters, batteries and propane tanks live), rather than at the lot gate, which the facility usually covers already.</p>

<h2>Mounting a camera on an RV without drilling</h2>
<p>Drilling through an RV’s skin risks water leaks, and many owners understandably refuse to do it. These no-drill options work, with limits.</p>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/rv-mounting.webp" alt="Camera mounting options on an RV including ladder clamp and magnetic base" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Ladder clamps and strap mounts are the most secure no-drill options on an RV.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Mount type</th><th>Where it works</th><th>Security</th><th>Limits</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Ladder clamp / pole clamp</td><td>Rear ladder, roof rack, spare-tire carrier</td><td>Good (add a cable lock)</td><td>Fixed to the rear; view is limited to one side</td></tr>
<tr><td>Magnetic base</td><td>Steel bumpers, steel frames, some steel-sided rigs</td><td>Low (easy to pull off)</td><td>Most RV walls are aluminum or fiberglass, so magnets will not stick</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suction cup</td><td>Inside of a window</td><td>Low</td><td>Not reliable outdoors in heat and cold; glass reflects IR at night</td></tr>
<tr><td>Awning arm clamp</td><td>Awning support arms</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Only when awning is out; arms move in wind</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strap mount</td><td>Trees, posts, picnic shelter beams at camp</td><td>Moderate with a lock</td><td>Depends on what is nearby</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tripod / stake</td><td>On the ground at camp</td><td>Low</td><td>Easy to knock over or take; useful for a temporary view</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A ladder clamp is the best all-round choice for a <strong>solar camera for travel trailer</strong> use: it is solid, sits high, and comes off in a minute. For more options, see our full guide to <a href="/how-to/install-without-drilling/">installing a security camera without drilling</a>.</p>
<div class="callout tip"><strong>Camera behind glass at night</strong><p>A camera inside the RV looking out through a window will see fine by day, but its infrared night vision bounces off the glass and blinds the image. Turn IR off and rely on outside lighting, or mount outside.</p></div>

<h2>12 V house-battery powered options</h2>
<p>Full-timers and long-stay campers sometimes prefer a camera wired into the RV’s 12 V system. These cameras run continuously, can record 24/7 to a local recorder, and never need recharging, as long as the house battery is topped up by your roof solar, shore power or generator.</p>
<p>The cost is power draw. A continuously recording 12 V camera commonly draws 2–5 W, which is 48–120 Wh a day. That is manageable on a rig with a few hundred watts of roof solar and a 100 Ah lithium house battery (about 1,280 Wh), and a real drain on a small lead-acid setup. A battery-and-solar camera, by contrast, may average a few Wh a day because it sleeps between events. The <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">solar camera battery calculator</a> lets you compare these budgets.</p>
<p>Wired 12 V cameras are also a permanent install. You are drilling or routing cables, which is fine for a backup camera or a full-timer’s security setup and overkill for weekend trips.</p>

<h2>How to choose solar cameras for RVs</h2>
<p>Once you know whether the camera is for camping, storage or both, a short checklist narrows the field.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Connectivity that matches the job.</strong> Wi-Fi for camping with a router; 4G LTE for storage; a dual Wi-Fi and LTE model if you want one camera for both.</li>
<li><strong>Local storage.</strong> A microSD slot means the camera keeps recording when there is no signal, which happens often on the road.</li>
<li><strong>Compact, light housing.</strong> Big floodlight cameras are awkward on a ladder clamp. A smaller body with a separate panel is easier to mount and stow.</li>
<li><strong>Panel cable length.</strong> A 3–5 m cable lets the panel sit on the roof or a sunny spot while the camera stays under the awning or in the shade.</li>
<li><strong>Weather and temperature rating.</strong> IP65 or better for rain and dust. Check the operating temperature range if you travel to deserts or store the rig through northern winters.</li>
<li><strong>Person detection on the camera.</strong> Busy campgrounds produce constant motion. On-camera person detection keeps alerts useful; check whether it needs a subscription.</li>
<li><strong>Quick release.</strong> A mount you can remove in under a minute is one you will actually remove before driving.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide compares camera categories in more detail.</p>

<h2>Keeping a storage camera charged through winter</h2>
<p>Many RVs sit in storage from late autumn to spring, exactly when solar output is lowest. In northern states, expect 2–3 peak sun hours a day in winter, and less if the panel is shaded by a neighboring fifth-wheel. Many lithium camera batteries also stop accepting charge below about 0 °C (32 °F).</p>
<p>The fix is to reduce demand and maximize sun. Tilt the panel steeply (roughly your latitude plus 15°) so snow slides off, turn off live-view habits, narrow the activity zone to your door, and pick a camera with an external panel rather than a small built-in one. Our <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-in-winter/">winter solar camera guide</a> explains the cold-charging limits in more detail.</p>

<h2>Connectivity: mobile routers and Starlink</h2>
<p>If your RV already has a mobile router or satellite internet, a Wi-Fi camera rides along for free. A few things to know:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mobile routers.</strong> A 4G/5G travel router gives all your devices one consistent network. Cameras set up once keep working at every stop. The router itself needs 12 V power, typically a few watts, so it runs from the house battery.</li>
<li><strong>Starlink and similar satellite internet.</strong> Works almost anywhere with a clear sky, but the dish and router draw far more power than a camera, often tens of watts continuously. Running it all night just to keep a camera online can drain a house battery. Many owners turn satellite internet off overnight and let the camera record locally to microSD.</li>
<li><strong>Phone hotspot.</strong> Fine for setup and for checking clips when you are at the rig; not useful for alerts when you leave with the phone.</li>
</ul>
<p>When none of these are running, a 4G camera with its own SIM is the only way to get alerts while you are away from the rig.</p>

<h2>Theft and tamper protection</h2>
<p>An RV camera is easy to see and often easy to reach, which makes it a target. A few habits help:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mount high on the ladder or roof rack, out of easy reach.</li>
<li>Run a thin cable lock through the camera’s mount and around the ladder rung.</li>
<li>Enable tamper or motion alarms if the camera has them, and lock the camera to your account.</li>
<li>Rely on uploads, not just the microSD card. If the camera is taken, the card goes with it.</li>
<li>Do not leave a camera running unattended at a campsite for days if you would not leave a bike there either.</li>
</ul>
<p>See <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-at-night/">do solar cameras work at night</a> for what to expect from night footage at a dark campground.</p>

<h2>Travel considerations: driving with a camera</h2>
<p>Treat the camera like your awning: stow it before you drive. Highway wind, vibration and road debris will loosen clamps, crack housings or tear the camera off entirely, and a camera hitting the car behind you is a real hazard.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Remove before departure</strong><p>Add the camera to your pre-drive checklist alongside the steps, antenna and awning.</p></li>
<li><strong>Stow it dry</strong><p>Keep the camera and panel in a padded bag in a cabinet so they do not rattle.</p></li>
<li><strong>Charge while driving</strong><p>If the battery is low, charge it from a USB port off the house or chassis battery on the road.</p></li>
<li><strong>Re-aim at each site</strong><p>Point the panel toward the sun (true south in the northern hemisphere) and check the camera view in the app. The <a href="/tools/solar-panel-angle-calculator/">panel angle calculator</a> helps if you are parked for a long stay.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>For long-term storage cameras, leave the camera on its mount in the lot, but take it off before pulling the rig out.</p>

<h2>Campsite security camera etiquette</h2>
<p>A <strong>campsite security camera</strong> pointed at your own gear is generally fine. Pointed at the neighboring site, the restroom path or a group campfire, it is a quick way to upset people and possibly break campground rules. Keep the view on your site, turn off audio recording (audio is subject to stricter consent laws in many states), and check the campground’s policies. Public land agencies and private parks set their own rules; a quick question at check-in avoids trouble.</p>
<p>For more on how cameras behave away from home, see <a href="/how-solar-cameras-work/">how solar cameras work</a> and our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide.</p>
